  "description": "The Divya Desams are the 108 holy abodes of Vishnu celebrated in the Tamil hymns of the twelve Alvars, the poet-saints of the Sri Vaishnava tradition, gathered in the Nalayira Divya Prabandham. To visit them is to follow the Alvars' own map of devotion. Tradition groups them by region: forty in the Chola country along the Kaveri, eighteen in the Pandya country, thirteen in Malai Nadu (Kerala and Kanyakumari), two in Nadu Nadu, twenty-two in Thondai Nadu around Kanchipuram, and eleven in the north — from Tirumala and Ahobilam to Badrinath, Dwarka and Muktinath in Nepal. The last two, the Milky Ocean and Paramapadam, Vishnu's supreme abode, lie beyond the earthly world, reached, the tradition says, only at the end of the soul's journey. All 106 earthly shrines are gathered here region by region, each region with its own map; at Tholaivillimangalam the twin temples of Irattai Tirupati together count as one.",
